      {
        "heading": "Governance at a glance: Organizations, Control Tower and SCPs",
        "content": "As soon as a company has more than a handful of accounts, the monitoring and billing practices above need a structure around them. Three services provide it.\n\n**AWS Organizations** groups accounts under a **management account** into a hierarchy of **organizational units (OUs)**. It provides consolidated billing (one invoice, shared discounts), centralized account creation through an API, and a place to attach policies. A common layout separates accounts by purpose and risk: a security account for logging and tooling, a shared-services account, and separate workload accounts for dev and prod. Separate accounts are the strongest isolation boundary AWS offers, because a compromise or a misconfiguration in one team's account does not automatically reach another's.\n\n**AWS Control Tower** automates setting up a multi-account environment following AWS best practices, called a **landing zone**. It builds on Organizations, creates a log archive and an audit account, enables organization-wide logging, and offers an **Account Factory** for provisioning new accounts with a standard baseline (often through CloudFormation or Service Catalog). It also applies **controls** (formerly guardrails): preventive controls implemented as service control policies and detective controls implemented as AWS Config rules, for example detect public S3 buckets or disallow disabling CloudTrail.\n\n**Service control policies (SCPs)** define the maximum permissions available in the accounts they attach to. They never grant permission; they only restrict what IAM policies in those accounts could otherwise allow, and they do not apply to the management account. The mechanics and example policies are covered in the existing cloud security lesson, so use that lesson when you need the detail of writing a deny statement or testing its effect.\n\nHow the pieces connect to this lesson:\n\n- **Billing:** Organizations gives the payer account one invoice and one place to view cost by member account; tags and Budgets then work at account or organization level.\n- **Monitoring:** organization trails and centralized log accounts collect CloudTrail and CloudWatch data from all accounts into one protected location.\n- **IaC:** Account Factory and StackSets deploy the same CloudFormation baseline into many accounts and Regions at once, so every new account starts with identical guardrails.\n- **Well-Architected:** Control Tower controls are a practical way to enforce security and operational pillars at scale.\n\nPitfalls: running production workloads in the management account (which SCPs cannot restrict and which holds billing power), building a deep OU tree that nobody understands, and applying a broad SCP deny directly to the root of the organization without testing it on a sandbox OU first. A badly written SCP can lock out every administrator."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Worked example: guardrails for a new team account",
        "content": "A new team receives an empty account. Before they deploy anything, the platform engineer applies a baseline with three guardrails: cost visibility (an AWS Budget), a security detection alarm (CloudWatch alarm on root login), and consistency (everything is defined in one CloudFormation template). The team's CloudTrail already writes to a CloudWatch Logs group, whose name is passed as a parameter.\n\n```yaml\nAWSTemplateFormatVersion: '2010-09-09'\nDescription: Team account guardrails baseline\nParameters:\n  AlertEmail:\n    Type: String\n  MonthlyLimit:\n    Type: Number\n    Default: 500\n  TrailLogGroup:\n    Type: String\nResources:\n  AlertTopic:\n    Type: AWS::SNS::Topic\n    Properties:\n      Subscription:\n        - Protocol: email\n          Endpoint: !Ref AlertEmail\n      Tags:\n        - Key: team\n          Value: platform\n  RootLoginFilter:\n    Type: AWS::Logs::MetricFilter\n    Properties:\n      LogGroupName: !Ref TrailLogGroup\n      FilterPattern: '{ ($.userIdentity.type = \"Root\") && ($.eventName = \"ConsoleLogin\") }'\n      MetricTransformations:\n        - MetricNamespace: Guardrails\n          MetricName: RootConsoleLogin\n          MetricValue: '1'\n  RootLoginAlarm:\n    Type: AWS::CloudWatch::Alarm\n    Properties:\n      AlarmName: root-console-login\n      Namespace: Guardrails\n      MetricName: RootConsoleLogin\n      Statistic: Sum\n      Period: 300\n      EvaluationPeriods: 1\n      Threshold: 1\n      ComparisonOperator: GreaterThanOrEqualToThreshold\n      TreatMissingData: notBreaching\n      AlarmActions:\n        - !Ref AlertTopic\n  MonthlyBudget:\n    Type: AWS::Budgets::Budget\n    Properties:\n      Budget:\n        BudgetName: team-monthly-cost\n        BudgetType: COST\n        TimeUnit: MONTHLY\n        BudgetLimit:\n          Amount: !Ref MonthlyLimit\n          Unit: USD\n      NotificationsWithSubscribers:\n        - Notification:\n            NotificationType: ACTUAL\n            ComparisonOperator: GREATER_THAN\n            Threshold: 80\n            ThresholdType: PERCENTAGE\n          Subscribers:\n            - SubscriptionType: EMAIL\n              Address: !Ref AlertEmail\n        - Notification:\n            NotificationType: FORECASTED\n            ComparisonOperator: GREATER_THAN\n            Threshold: 100\n            ThresholdType: PERCENTAGE\n          Subscribers:\n            - SubscriptionType: EMAIL\n              Address: !Ref AlertEmail\n```\n\n**Explaining each guardrail:**\n\n- **SNS topic:** the single notification channel. The email subscriber must confirm the subscription, otherwise alarm messages go nowhere. In production, point it at a shared mailbox or a chat integration, not a personal address.\n- **Metric filter plus alarm:** CloudTrail is the source of truth that a root console login happened, and CloudWatch turns that record into a number and a notification. This is the CloudWatch and CloudTrail partnership from earlier. TreatMissingData notBreaching is correct here because no logins means no data points, which is the healthy state.\n- **Budget with two notifications:** the 80 percent actual alert says you are close to the limit, while the 100 percent forecasted alert fires earlier in the month when the trend says you will exceed it. Forecast-based alerts need a few days of usage history to work.\n\n**Deployment procedure:** commit the template, run cfn-lint in CodeBuild, create a change set for review, execute it, then run drift detection weekly. Enable termination protection on the stack, and activate the team tag as a cost allocation tag in the Billing console so the Budget and Cost Explorer can group by it.\n\n**What this baseline does not cover:** it does not stop spending (Budgets only alert unless you configure a budget action), it does not prevent root use (an SCP or simply protecting the root credentials with MFA does that), and it does not replace a Well-Architected review. It is the minimum that guarantees someone hears about the two events that hurt most in a new account: unexpected cost and unexpected root access."
      }
