        "heading": "Worked mini-report: a dropper that persists and beacons",
        "content": "Suppose the analysis VM session produced these observations for a file received by email as invoice-viewer.exe. In Process Explorer, the process was started by explorer.exe, was unsigned, and showed a VirusTotal score of 41/72. In Procmon, the process created a file in a user-writable folder, wrote a value to the HKCU Run key whose data pointed to that file, and then started the dropped copy. In TCPView, the dropped copy opened a connection to a remote host over port 443 every 60 seconds. Sigcheck reported entropy of 7.4, and Strings showed a domain name and a mutex name.\n\nStep one is to convert observations into an IOC table. Each row has a type, a value, a role, its source and a confidence level.\n\n| Type | Value | Role | Source | Confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SHA-256 | [full 64-character hash of invoice-viewer.exe] | Initial dropper | Sigcheck | High |\n| File path | C:\\Users\\Public\\svcsync.exe | Dropped persistent copy | Procmon | High |\n| Registry value | HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\\OneSync | Persistence, data points to dropped file | Procmon, Autoruns | High |\n| Mutex | Global\\syncmtx9f2a | Single-instance marker | Handle, Strings | Medium |\n| Domain | cdn-sync[.]example[.]net | Command-and-control | Strings, TCPView | High |\n| IP address | 203.0.113.45 | Resolved C2 address at time of test | TCPView | Medium, may rotate |\n| Network pattern | HTTPS to the domain every 60 seconds | Beaconing interval | TCPView | Medium |\n\nStep two is to map behaviours to ATT&CK, each with its evidence: User Execution: Malicious File (T1204.002) for the email delivery; Boot or Logon Autostart Execution: Registry Run Keys (T1547.001) for the OneSync value; Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols (T1071.001) for the HTTPS beaconing; Obfuscated Files or Information: Software Packing (T1027.002) for the entropy and purple process.\n\nStep three is the executive summary. Here is a version that passes the 'no tool names, no hashes' test:\n\n'The file invoice-viewer.exe is a malicious downloader delivered by email. When opened, it copies itself to a public folder, configures itself to restart every time the user logs on, and contacts an external server once a minute, which would give an attacker a channel to send further instructions. We have high confidence in this behaviour, confirmed by three independent tools. We did not test what it downloads next because the lab had no internet access. We recommend blocking the listed domain and address at the proxy and firewall, searching endpoints for the listed file path and registry value, and resetting the credentials of any user who ran the file.'\n\nStep four is detection guidance: a hunting query for RegSetValue or registry events on the Run key with data pointing at a Public folder, a proxy query for the beacon domain and its interval, and a reference to the YARA rule that matches the mutex and the domain string. Step five is recommendations, ordered as contain, eradicate (delete the file and the Run value), recover and harden (restrict execution from user-writable folders). Notice that every sentence in the summary can be traced to a row in the table, which in turn is traceable to a capture. That traceability is the mark of a professional report."
